and they're going to realize it's even less than that.

165
00:10:59,280 --> 00:11:02,260
And God actually named every one of them.

166
00:11:03,480 --> 00:11:06,300
I mean, I've got four kids, and I can't keep up with them sometimes.

167
00:11:06,300 --> 00:11:10,300
You know, Cassie, guys, whatever your name is, son, go there.

168
00:11:10,300 --> 00:11:19,100
And so the question begs, what goes on God's refrigerator?

169
00:11:19,100 --> 00:11:23,860
There is a Hebrew word for special.

170
00:11:24,800 --> 00:11:26,540
We don't usually translate it special.

171
00:11:26,540 --> 00:11:29,080
We translate it holy.

172
00:11:29,080 --> 00:11:32,480
But you do know that holy means set apart.

173
00:11:32,480 --> 00:11:37,520
That we think of holy as ethically pure, and it is that.

174
00:11:37,520 --> 00:11:43,720
We're ethically pure, holy, ethically pure, because we're set apart.

175
00:11:44,540 --> 00:11:49,820
There are vessels in the temple, in the old temple, in Solomon's temple, that were holy bowls.

176
00:11:49,820 --> 00:11:54,140
They weren't more ethically pure than the bowl that's in my kitchen sink.

177
00:11:54,140 --> 00:11:55,920
But they're special.

178
00:11:55,920 --> 00:11:58,200
They're set apart.

179
00:11:59,360 --> 00:12:07,380
And so when God finishes all of this amazing creation, the very first thing he calls holy

180
00:12:07,380 --> 00:12:12,240
is actually not a thing at all.

181
00:12:12,240 --> 00:12:16,980
But it was Sabbath, the seventh day.

182
00:12:16,980 --> 00:12:23,800
Abraham Heschel says that God created his sanctuary not in place, but in time.

183
00:12:24,980 --> 00:12:28,800
But God uniquely set apart time.

184
00:12:28,800 --> 00:12:31,300
And I want to unpack that in Genesis.

185
00:12:31,300 --> 00:12:36,640
But before I do, just so many of you don't know me, I'm going to talk about Sabbath.

186
00:12:36,640 --> 00:12:40,160
But I don't want to come at it from a legalistic perspective.

187
00:12:40,160 --> 00:12:45,080
I don't care if you think you can go to a restaurant on Sabbath or not, or if you think you can drive

188
00:12:45,080 --> 00:12:48,520
or not, or if you think it's Saturday or Sunday or Tuesday.

189
00:12:50,420 --> 00:13:00,880
But God, in his fabric of creation, created this rhythm of regular time and special time.

190
00:13:00,880 --> 00:13:07,140
And when he calls something special, something holy first, the first thing he calls holy is,

191
00:13:07,140 --> 00:13:08,100
in fact, Sabbath.

192
00:13:08,100 --> 00:13:13,680
In fact, there is no thing called holy in the Old Testament until the golden calf.

193
00:13:14,920 --> 00:13:23,040
It's almost as though the qualification of things is an accommodation to you and I who are so into thinghood.

194
00:13:23,040 --> 00:13:29,940
If you look at Latin, the word for thing is res, where we get our word reality.

195
00:13:29,940 --> 00:13:32,680
You know, our reality is based in thinghood.

196
00:13:32,680 --> 00:13:33,520
It's how we think.

197
00:13:33,520 --> 00:13:34,460
It's how we function.

198
00:13:34,460 --> 00:13:37,820
But in Hebrew, there's actually no word for thing.

199
00:13:38,840 --> 00:13:43,160
Now, some will argue the bar, but the bar is not thing like object.

200
00:13:43,160 --> 00:13:46,320
It's not thing like object.

201
00:13:46,320 --> 00:13:51,820
And I'm not a Hebrew scholar, but Abraham Heschel is, and that's where I found it out.

202
00:13:51,820 --> 00:13:57,700
God has set aside time.

203
00:13:59,180 --> 00:14:05,000
You know, if you look at Genesis, I know a lot of people want to really spend some time on Genesis,

204
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:10,020
talking about, you know, young earth, old earth, what is it?

205
00:14:10,020 --> 00:14:14,260
I've met people for whom that's a litmus test of your salvation.

206
00:14:14,260 --> 00:14:15,160
It's not for me.

207
00:14:15,160 --> 00:14:21,420
But it is fascinating that the whole structure of Genesis chapter 1 through, well, the first creation,

208
00:14:21,420 --> 00:14:29,120
the creation account, Genesis 1, 1 through Genesis 2, verse 3, is actually comprised of seven sections.

209
00:14:29,120 --> 00:14:35,280
The first sentence in that passage, that section, seven words.

210
00:14:35,280 --> 00:14:40,960
The second sentence, 14 words, multiple of seven.

211
00:14:40,960 --> 00:14:49,360
In fact, every major word in that whole passage, words like God and earth and heaven and light,

212
00:14:49,360 --> 00:14:52,880
show up in multiples of seven.

213
00:14:52,880 --> 00:14:59,180
You know, so I think we often don't realize it because we're such a written culture.

214
00:14:59,180 --> 00:15:05,160
And really, in two of the Gutenberg Bible, there weren't a lot of written primary cultures.

215
00:15:05,160 --> 00:15:06,120
There were oral cultures.

216
00:15:06,120 --> 00:15:09,500
And even though you had writing, you didn't read it.

217
00:15:09,500 --> 00:15:10,600
It was read to you.

218
00:15:10,600 --> 00:15:12,340
You heard it.

219
00:15:12,340 --> 00:15:19,240
And so much of the Old Testament is written in ways that actually allow you to understand

220
00:15:19,240 --> 00:15:19,960
the entirety.

221
00:15:19,960 --> 00:15:25,840
And in the forming of it, it emphasizes the things that it wants you to understand.

222
00:15:25,840 --> 00:15:28,120
It's really interesting.

223
00:15:28,120 --> 00:15:33,700
The final section of this whole creation story, the seventh section, which is the climax of it,

224
00:15:33,700 --> 00:15:35,880
has three sentences.

225
00:15:35,880 --> 00:15:40,120
And each sentence has seven words.

226
00:15:40,980 --> 00:15:46,920
And the middle word for each one of those three sentences is seventh day.

227
00:15:46,920 --> 00:15:54,320
There was a debate among Jewish scholars way back when, when they were still using berry juice on papyra.

228
00:15:54,820 --> 00:16:00,920
And they were writing back and forth and arguing about what does Genesis 2, 2 really mean?

229
00:16:01,560 --> 00:16:06,840
Because the actual language, and a lot of our translations try to, try to make it understandable.

